1
00:04:43,333 --> 00:04:49,333
Black Is King is a music film directed by Beyoncé.

2
00:09:26,666 --> 00:09:32,666
This visual album from Beyoncé reimagines the lessons of "The Lion King" (2019)
for today's young kings and queens in search of their own crowns.

3
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:16,000
Black Is King is a 2020 American musical film co-written,
executive produced, and directed by Beyoncé.

4
00:18:53,333 --> 00:18:59,333
It is a visual companion to the 2019 album The Lion King: The Gift,
curated by Beyoncé for the film The Lion King (2019).

5
00:23:36,666 --> 00:23:42,666
The film tells the story of a young African prince (Folajomi Akinmurele)
who is exiled from his kingdom after his father's death.

6
00:28:20,000 --> 00:28:26,000
As he grows into a man (Nyaniso Dzedze), he undergoes a journey of self-identity, using the guidance of his ancestor
(Beyoncé), childhood love (Nandi Madida), and his own subconscious (Stephen Ojo) to reclaim his throne.

7
00:33:03,333 --> 00:33:09,333
The prince's journey acts as an allegory for the African diaspora's journey of discovering, reclaiming and celebrating their culture
and heritage, which is echoed by the inclusion of spoken-word poetry that focuses on the question of black identity.

8
00:37:46,666 --> 00:37:52,666
Black Is King was in production for over a year,
across six countries on three continents.

9
00:42:30,000 --> 00:42:36,000
Beyoncé wanted to recruit a diverse cast and crew and to
provide opportunities for new talent.

10
00:47:13,333 --> 00:47:19,333
The music, dances, costumes, hairstyles, and sets were designed to showcase the beauty
and richness of the cultures in the African continent and diaspora.

11
00:51:56,666 --> 00:52:02,666
The film was released globally on Disney+ on July 31, 2020, and was aired the following day across
Sub-Saharan Africa on M-Net and Canal+ Afrique and across the Middle East and North Africa on OSN.

12
00:56:40,000 --> 00:56:46,000
An extended version of "Black Parade" was used for the film's credits, and was
included in a deluxe edition of The Gift released alongside the film.

13
01:01:23,333 --> 01:01:29,333
The film received praise for Beyoncé's direction, the cinematography,
score, costume design, subject matter, and cultural themes.

14
01:06:06,666 --> 01:06:12,666
It received six nominations at the 63rd Annual Grammy
Awards, including Best Music Film.

15
01:10:50,000 --> 01:10:56,000
"Brown Skin Girl" won the award for Best Music Video, while "Black
Parade" won the award for Best R&B Performance.

16
01:15:33,333 --> 01:15:39,333
It was Beyoncé's 28th Grammy win, making her the most awarded
singer and female artist in Grammy history.

17
01:20:16,666 --> 01:20:22,666
The film also won the Primetime Emmy Award for Outstanding Costumes for a Variety,
Nonfiction, or Reality Program at the 73rd Primetime Emmy Awards.
